Reticulum Vector Corpus

What this is

Each vector is a directory holding input bytes, the expected field decomposition, and a record of where the data came from. An implementation checks itself by decoding the input and comparing its output to the expected decomposition.

This is not a specification. It does not define correct behaviour. It records the observed behaviour of one implementation at one version:

    python-rns 1.4.2, commit b48b96e61676504e0a4e527b33b9a0b4495c6872,
    openssl backend

Every vector records that pin. When the pin moves, vectors are regenerated and the differences are examined.


Checking an implementation

    make
    cmd/check

runs the corpus against cmd/dump. To check your own implementation, make it print the same field format and substitute it for dump:

    DUMP=./my-implementation cmd/check

A decoder that implements only the decode direction can skip the round trip described below:

    DUMP=./my-implementation ENCODE=no cmd/check

It gives up most of what the corpus can tell it. Decoding proves an implementation reads what python-rns writes; the round trip is the only thing here that says anything about what it writes.

doc/harness is the whole of what a program has to do to be substituted for dump here, including which kinds it may leave out and how that appears in the result, and cmd/dump -l lists the kinds it can be asked for. It also shows how to run this from an implementation's own CI.

The corpus number is a checksum over Test/INDEX, every raw and expect. It says what the result was measured against. A count alone does not: two vectors have changed shape without changing the count, and any number recorded elsewhere against the old shape would still have looked current. Record it beside any result kept outside this repository.


One vector

Every vector is three files, and the smallest kind shows the shape. Test/identity/upstream-0 is a public key and what is derived from it:

    $ cmd/dump identity test/identity/upstream-0/raw
    public_key         4219d9ce29da42bc823b66b24a051c65...c616501b
    x25519_public      4219d9ce29da42bc823b66b24a051c65...62924930
    ed25519_public     1cd07b674027511e73469c4edec93634...c616501b
    identity_hash      650b5d76b6bec0390d1f8cfca5bd33f9

That output is test/identity/upstream-0/expect, byte for byte, and that is the whole contract. A decoder passes a vector when

    my-decoder <kind> test/<kind>/<name>/raw

prints the expect file. Where the determinism class is encode, cmd/check also runs

    my-decoder -e <kind> test/<kind>/<name>/expect

and requires raw back.

The hex above is shortened to fit this page. Nothing in the format wraps or elides, so read a vector rather than a description of one:

    cat test/pathrequest/tagged/*

That one is a whole packet and uses no cryptography. Its expect file holds the eleven header fields every packet kind carries, then five of its own. doc/fields is the index of all of them.


Vector format

    test/INDEX          every vector in the corpus, one per line

    test/<kind>/<name>/

        meta      provenance and determinism class
        raw       input, hex, one blob per line
        expect    field decomposition, one field per line

cmd/check refuses to run against a test/ that does not hold exactly what INDEX lists, and names what is missing.

meta records these fields, spelled in lower case as shown:

    source        which implementation and version produced this
    determinism   decode or encode
    purpose       one line

cmd/check requires all three and rejects an unknown determinism class.

A vector of the encode class carries in expect everything raw contains, so raw can be rebuilt from it; cmd/check does exactly that and diffs the result against raw. Nearly every vector is of that class. Those that are not name their reason:

    test/signature/, test/sign/   expect records the 950-byte message
                                  by length and digest, not by content
    the rejections                the bytes that broke the rule are not
                                  printed, so expect names the rule and
                                  the numbers behind it instead

A broken rule does not by itself put a vector in the decode class. short-plaintext is broken inside a plaintext the packet around it decoded whole, so expect holds every byte of raw; test/linkdata holds four of those. See doc/fields, section Rejection.

The class says whether expect is total, not whether the reference could produce these bytes a second time. Most Reticulum objects contain randomness and cannot be produced twice; expect records what that randomness came out as, and raw follows from it.

An object whose input is not the object itself carries that input in expect too, so that expect stays total: the recipient key of an encrypted packet, the link request a proof answers. Every such value is already a line of raw. See doc/.

Every value in expect is one of three things: hex, a decimal number, or a keyword from a fixed set. Byte strings are always hex, names included. 

No value is ever empty. Hex cannot spell the empty byte string, and a field name followed by nothing is a line with a trailing space that cannot be told from a truncated one, so the empty byte string is written "-" like every other absent value.

The number of hex lines in raw depends on the kind and is documented in doc/.

An object that cannot be decoded yields the rule it broke and the numbers behind it:

    invalid            short-payload
    payload_length     147
    minimum_length     148


Layout

    CONFORMANCE results of running the corpus against other implementations
    conformance/ the harnesses those results were produced with
    doc/        byte layouts and derivation rules; start at doc/fields, and at doc/harness to check your own implementation
    test/       the vectors
    cmd/        dump, a decoder written in C; check, a shell script
    tools/      gen, the Python generator

A user needs test/, doc/ and cmd/. The other two are the larger half of the repository and neither is part of the corpus: tools/ needs Python and the pinned checkout, and conformance/ needs the compiler for the languages the harness is implemented in.

doc/fields is the index of every name an expect file can carry, what it holds and which kinds carry it. The vectors are the authority on which names exist.

dump only needs a C compiler. SHA-256, HMAC-SHA256, HKDF and AES-256-CBC decryption are written out in cmd/; tweetnacl supplies Ed25519 signing and verification, SHA-512 and X25519 and is vendored unmodified, see cmd/VENDOR.


Where raw and expect come from

raw is what the reference produced, or configuration a human would have typed. No line of it is a value tools/gen composed out of the reference's own functions. See doc/packet.

expect is tools/gen's own reading of raw, and cmd/dump reads raw again without sharing a line of code with it.

The backend is part of the pin because python-rns ships two curve implementations, picks one at import time by what is installed, and they do not agree everywhere. Vectors turn on the difference, and tools/gen refuses to run under the other one. Which vectors is not counted here: see doc/identity, section "Canonical signatures", and doc/encryption, section "Contributory behaviour", which name them.

gen needs Python and a checkout of the pinned reference implementation. It looks for one in ../upstream and takes --checkout PATH otherwise. Consumers of the corpus need neither.

    make verify

runs check and additionally regenerates every vector from the pinned reference implementation and fails if a single byte would change, and checks the vendored file against the hashes in cmd/VENDOR.

doc/ and the programs name the reference by symbol:

    RNS/Packet.py#get_hashable_part

The anchor is the name to grep for.


Every private key here is public

Five kinds carry a private key in every one of their vectors: test/keyset/ and test/sign/ carry an identity key, test/encrypted/ the recipient's, test/linkdata/ the responder's link key, and test/ifac/ the interface key, which is derived rather than given and no less public for it. The ones named upstream are lifted from the upstream test suite; the rest are generated from a fixed seed or derived from a published network name and passphrase. They are published, in this repository, on purpose, because a decryption vector cannot be checked without one.

Never load one into anything that talks to a network. An identity built from a key in this corpus can be impersonated by anyone who has read this corpus, and every destination derived from it is already known.


Known limitation

Vectors generated from python-rns cannot detect bugs in python-rns. They define "compatible" as "identical to the reference implementation".


Relationship to the upstream test suite

The upstream suite is a regression suite. It builds objects with RNS and validates them with RNS, and asserts nothing about the resulting bytes. It cannot detect an incompatibility in a different implementation.

Where upstream does provide reusable values, they are adopted here verbatim and their origin recorded in meta, rather than re-derived.

Primitives that every language already provides are not tested here.


Upstream license

Reticulum is licensed permissively with two added restrictions: no use in systems intended to harm humans, and no use in AI or language model training datasets. Adopted values are attributed in the meta file of the vector that carries them.
